a = 2 recovers\n    # the refined model): small kappa, only grossly lopsided coins imagined.\n    log_kappa = pm.Normal(\"log_kappa\", mu=math.log(2.0), sigma=0.75)\n    kappa = pm.Deterministic(\"kappa\", pt.exp(log_kappa))\n    a_h = kappa * s_heads\n    a_t = kappa - a_h\n    seq_lbias = (\n        pt.gammaln(seq_heads + a_h) + pt.gammaln(seq_len - seq_heads + a_t)\n        - pt.gammaln(seq_len + kappa)\n        - pt.gammaln(a_h) - pt.gammaln(a_t) + pt.gammaln(kappa)\n    )'''\nassert old in s; s=s.replace(old,new,1)\nopen(p,\"w\").write(s)\nEOF\ncat > $D/model_name.txt <<'EOF'\nlopsided_trick_coin_probit\nEOF\ncat > $D/hypothesis.md <<'EOF'\nRefinement of `thurstonian_probit_decision` (people pick the sequence whose noisy felt randomness is larger — a probit comparison — where felt randomness is how much better their own second-order picture of a fair coin explains the sequence than the regular generators they suspect: a switch-biased coin, a heads-favoured trick coin and a repeating motif with slips, weighed per flip with pseudo-flip dilution, heavy-tailed signed person sensitivity and a side habit). The single change is in how lopsided people imagine a trick coin to be: instead of a fixed prior over its heads rate, how concentrated that prior is is fitted, so if people picture trick coins as grossly rigged, only an extreme heads/tails imbalance is explained away as rigging, and a moderately lopsided but frequently switching sequence still looks random. This addresses the critique that in long pairs where the more-switching sequence is also the more lopsided one, people choose the more-switching sequence more often than the best model predicts — they weigh heads/tails balance against switching less than it assumes.\nEOF\ncd /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/runs/run3/repo && /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/venv/bin/python -m src.pipelines.inner_loop.check_candidate --candidate-dir /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_li