{"type":"system","subtype":"background_tasks_changed","tasks":[{"task_id":"bxbw2dsrt","task_type":"local_bash","description":"cd /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop; D=iter_4/candidate_5; ls $D; cp models/thurstonian_probit_decision.py $D/candidate.py; python3 - <<'EOF'\np=\"iter_4/candidate_5/candidate.py\"\ns=open(p).read()\nold_head='''\"\"\"Thurstonian (Gaussian-noise, probit) decision rule on the incumbent's evidence.\n'''\nnew_head='''\"\"\"Fitted-concentration trick coin in the Thurstonian (probit) randomness judgment.\n\nRefinement of `thurstonian_probit_decision` (described below). Single change:\nthe trick coin people suspect no longer has a fixed-concentration prior over its\nheads rate, Beta(2 s, 2 (1 - s)), but Beta(kappa s, kappa (1 - s)) with the\nconcentration kappa fitted. kappa < 2 means people imagine trick coins as\ngrossly lopsided, so only extreme head/tail imbalance is explained away as\nrigging and a moderately lopsided but switchy sequence is still credited as\nrandom; kappa > 2 means they imagine mildly biased coins. Addresses the critique\nthat in length-8 pairs where the more-switching sequence is also the more\nlopsided one, people choose the more-switching sequence more often than the\nmodel predicts (they weigh heads/tails balance against switching less).\n\nDescription of the refined model follows.\n\nThurstonian (Gaussian-noise, probit) decision rule on the incumbent's evidence.\n'''\nassert old_head in s; s=s.replace(old_head,new_head,1)\nold='''    a_h = 2.0 * s_heads\n    a_t = 2.0 - a_h\n    seq_lbias = (\n        pt.gammaln(seq_heads + a_h) + pt.gammaln(seq_len - seq_heads + a_t)\n        - pt.gammaln(seq_len + 2.0)\n        - pt.gammaln(a_h) - pt.gammaln(a_t) + math.lgamma(2.0)\n    )'''\nnew='''    # Fitted concentration kappa of the trick-coin prior (kappa = 2 recovers\n    # the refined model): small kappa, only grossly lopsided coins imagined.\n    log_kappa = pm.Normal(\"log_kappa\", mu=math.log(2.0), sigma=0.75)\n    kappa = pm.Deterministic(\"kappa\", pt.exp(log_kappa))\n    a_h = kappa * s_heads\n    a_t = kappa - a_h\n    seq_lbias = (\n        pt.gammaln(seq_heads + a_h) + pt.gammaln(seq_len - seq_heads + a_t)\n        - pt.gammaln(seq_len + kappa)\n        - pt.gammaln(a_h) - pt.gammaln(a_t) + pt.gammaln(kappa)\n    )'''\nassert old in s; s=s.replace(old,new,1)\nopen(p,\"w\").write(s)\nEOF\ncat > $D/model_name.txt <<'EOF'\nlopsided_trick_coin_probit\nEOF\ncat > $D/hypothesis.md <<'EOF'\nRefinement of `thurstonian_probit_decision` (people pick the sequence whose noisy felt randomness is larger — a probit comparison — where felt randomness is how much better their own second-order picture of a fair coin explains the sequence than the regular generators they suspect: a switch-biased coin, a heads-favoured trick coin and a repeating motif with slips, weighed per flip with pseudo-flip dilution, heavy-tailed signed person sensitivity and a side habit). The single change is in how lopsided people imagine a trick coin to be: instead of a fixed prior over its heads rate, how concentrated that prior is is fitted, so if people picture trick coins as grossly rigged, only an extreme heads/tails imbalance is explained away as rigging, and a moderately lopsided but frequently switching sequence still looks random. This addresses the critique that in long pairs where the more-switching sequence is also the more lopsided one, people choose the more-switching sequence more often than the best model predicts — they weigh heads/tails balance against switching less than it assumes.\nEOF\ncd /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/runs/run3/repo && /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/venv/bin/python -m src.pipelines.inner_loop.check_candidate --candidate-dir /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/iter_4/candidate_5 --responses /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/responses.csv 2>&1 | tail -5"}],"uuid":"43eaf526-fe62-4408-908a-f5a2c994483d","session_id":"a0dbf71e-e1bc-43be-ae66-ad771a199989"}
